<PageTitle>Two-minute crew briefing — Interlock Expeditions</PageTitle>
<article class="prose"><p class="eyebrow">Before the clock starts</p><h1>Two cards are better than one.</h1><p>Interlock Expeditions is a cooperative game for two crews. One or more people can use each crew's console. Bring at least two devices with internet access and a way to talk: sit together or use your usual call. The site itself does not record a microphone or provide chat.</p>
<h2>A host's first room</h2><ol><li><a href="/account">Create a free host account</a> with an alias. Save its private recovery key; it replaces an email/password recovery process.</li><li><a href="/host">Choose a ready episode</a>. Its introduction and suggested duration are supplied. A longer clock can help first-time players.</li><li>Open the host controls, then open a machine crew console. Send the room invitation to the other device and ask that player to choose the signal crew.</li><li>Both players choose aliases. The host starts when at least one real player has joined each crew. A host who wants to play can keep the host controls in a separate tab.</li></ol>
<h2>How a station works</h2><p>Read your private crew card aloud when helpful. It often describes a setting controlled by the other crew. Your own card alone is intentionally not the whole briefing. The shared machine is real server state: changing a control on one console changes what the other crew reads after the next refresh.</p><p>At the first five stations, both crews press <strong>Check station</strong> after the settings are ready. A setting change clears earlier checks. A failed joint check or a blocked trolley move records a mistake and removes five seconds. The host can reset the current station for one mistake.</p><p>The final safety station uses two fifteen-second readiness windows. Agree before arming your own terminal. The server checks the overlap; refreshing a page does not restart either clock.</p>
<h2>The six kinds of machine</h2><ul><li><strong>Dock dials:</strong> machine crew sets three values; signal crew sets a route. Follow the other crew's instruction.</li><li><strong>Relay lattice:</strong> signal inputs travel through crossed wires. Machine bypass switches flip individual incoming lamps; the outputs are calculated.</li><li><strong>Collection trolley:</strong> signal crew sees the full four-by-four floor; machine crew moves the trolley and sees visited cells. Take the crate before reaching the exit.</li><li><strong>Freight cradle:</strong> machine crew loads all crates; signal crew controls ballast. The two calculated loads must match without exceeding the limit. More than one valid arrangement may work.</li><li><strong>Weather seal:</strong> each crew turns one ring. The other card names the required alignment.</li><li><strong>Safety terminals:</strong> both crews arm within overlapping windows. This is coordination, not a reaction-time contest.</li></ul>
<h2>If a device disconnects</h2><p>Keep its room tab if possible, then use Read current room state. A private player capability is saved per tab with a device-local fallback, and the server keeps the shared room. The host can pause when someone needs time, and a late guest joins at the current station. A removed player cannot keep reading or operating the room.</p><h2>Play fair, and keep private keys private</h2><p>These original episodes are fictional and their source is public. Solutions are not marketed as cryptographically hidden. To enjoy the cooperative puzzle, keep the cards on separate devices. Account recovery keys and private player capabilities are separate from fictional puzzle answers and should never be included in invitations.</p><div class="toolbar"><a class="button primary" href="/host">Open a ready expedition</a><a class="button" href="/">Browse episode stories</a></div></article>
